  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+49y=0,\\,y(\\pi )=-2,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(\\pi )=7.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-49 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 7\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}\u20617x+Q\\operatorname{sin}\u20617x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi )=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-P=-2\\,\u27f9\\,P=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(\\pi )=7,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-7P\\operatorname{sin}\u20617x+7Q\\operatorname{cos}\u20617x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(\\pi )=7$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7Q=7\\,\u27f9\\,Q=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}\u20617x-\\operatorname{sin}\u20617x.\n$$",
